      "math_academy_id": "e-486",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division. \n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{ 2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3} .\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
